    {
      "page_title": "Motivating Example: Money Transfer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Problem",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Transferring $100 between two accounts stored as separate documents. Debit and credit must both land, or the money vanishes."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Account A and Account B are separate documents",
            "Debit A by $100, Credit B by $100",
            "Without atomicity, a crash leaves one side updated"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Naive Approach",
          "ink": "orange"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "await accounts.updateOne(",
            "  { _id: 'A' },",
            "  { $inc: { balance: -100 } })",
            "// crash here → B never credited",
            "await accounts.updateOne(",
            "  { _id: 'B' },",
            "  { $inc: { balance: 100 } })"
          ],
          "note": "Crash between ops = lost money"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Why this fails",
          "text": "Each update is atomic on its own, but the pair is not. A stepdown or network blip between the two writes leaves the system inconsistent."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Transaction Fix",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const session = client.startSession()",
            "await session.withTransaction(async () => {",
            "  await accounts.updateOne(",
            "    { _id: 'A' },",
            "    { $inc: { balance: -100 } },",
            "    { session })",
            "  await accounts.updateOne(",
            "    { _id: 'B' },",
            "    { $inc: { balance: 100 } },",
            "    { session })",
            "})"
          ],
          "note": "Both land or neither does"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Session passed to every operation",
            "Commit is atomic across both documents",
            "Rollback on any failure restores both balances"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. What You Get",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Guarantee",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Atomicity",
              "all or nothing"
            ],
            [
              "Consistency",
              "balance sum preserved"
            ],
            [
              "Isolation",
              "snapshot read"
            ],
            [
              "Durability",
              "committed = durable"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Cost of This Example",
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two documents, one collection, one shard",
            "Single-shard txn: no 2PC overhead",
            "Snapshot held for the duration of both writes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The whole point",
          "text": "This is the minimum case where a transaction earns its cost. Two documents, one logical unit of work, no cross-service boundary. If you cannot justify this, you do not need a transaction."
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Atomicity in practice"
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of MONGODB TRANSACTIONS. MongoDB gives you atomicity for free inside one document — the moment you need two, you inherit sessions, snapshots, write conflicts and a two-phase commit. This handbook is the whole path: the core API, read and write concern, why snapshot isolation still lets write skew through, what changes on a sharded cluster, and the two error labels that decide whether you retry the transaction or just the commit. Save it for the day a transfer has to land on both sides.",
